To prevent BGP routes from being reflected back to members of the same neighbor group on Nokia 7750 SR route reflectors, configure a unique `cluster` ID at the neighbor level instead of the group level. This ensures each client is treated as an independent cluster, preventing inadvertent route reflection within the group while maintaining proper distribution to other peers.

```cli
configure router bgp
    group "CLIENT_GROUP"
        neighbor <client_ip_address>
            cluster <unique_cluster_id>
        exit
    exit
exit
```

**Explanation:**  
- Replace `CLIENT_GROUP` with your BGP group name.  
- For each client under this group, specify a `<unique_cluster_id>` (e.g., distinct IP address like `192.0.2.1`, `192.0.2.2`).  
- By assigning a unique cluster ID per neighbor, routes from one client **will not** be reflected to other clients in the same group, as they belong to separate clusters.  
- Routes will still be distributed to non-group peers (e.g., route reflector clients in other clusters, non-client peers).  

**Example for two clients:**  
```cli
configure router bgp
    group "CORE_CLIENTS"
        neighbor 203.0.113.10
            cluster 203.0.113.10
        exit
        neighbor 203.0.113.20
            cluster 203.0.113.20
        exit
    exit
exit
```  

This configuration isolates reflection domains per client while preserving standard BGP route distribution rules.
